  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 152,
      "text": "MEXICO\nAnnex XIV. Mexico’s Trade with Central America\nMexico’s trade with Central America is surprisingly low despite geographical proximity. Important\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 152,
      "end_char": 371,
      "text": "gains could be made by further trade integration through converging trade rules and regulatory\nstandards, strengthening infrastructure and human capital, reducing non-tariff barriers, and\nenhancing customs cooperation.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 371,
      "end_char": 552,
      "text": "1. Despite geographical proximity and a regional free trade agreement, Mexico’s trade\nwith Central America is very limited (Annex XIV Figure 1). Mexico’s exports to Central America\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 552,
      "end_char": 749,
      "text": "constitute a mere 1.7 percent of its total exports in 2021, while the country’s imports from Central\nAmerica comprise 0.6 percent of total imports. Similarly, Central America’s exposure to Mexico,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 749,
      "end_char": 941,
      "text": "though has increased over time, is low with around 5.8 percent of the region’s total exports going to\nMexico in 2021 and 13.5 percent of the imports coming from Mexico. In terms of individual\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 941,
      "end_char": 1139,
      "text": "countries, Nicaragua, Guatemala and Belize have the highest exposure in the region, with more than\n8 percent of exports going to Mexico. Notably, Nicaragua’s export exposure increased substantially\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1139,
      "end_char": 1373,
      "text": "following Mexico-Central America free trade agreement (signed in 2011 and implemented in 2013).\nThe import exposure of Guatemala, El Salvador and Honduras are the highest with more than 15\npercent of their imports coming from Mexico.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1373,
      "end_char": 1610,
      "text": "Annex XIV. Figure 1. Mexico’s Trade with Central America\nMexico Exports Mexico Imports\n100 (Percent of total exports) 0.6 100 (Percent of total imports) 1.0\n80 0.5 80 0 0 . . 8 9\n0.4 0.7\n60 60 0.6\n0.3 0.5\n40 40 0.4\n0.2\n0.3\n20 0.1 20 0.2\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1610,
      "end_char": 1796,
      "text": "0.1\n0 0.0 0 0.0\n2005 2007 2009 2011 2013 2015 2017 2019 2021 2005 2007 2009 2011 2013 2015 2017 2019 2021\nUSA (LHS) Costa Rica Belize El Salvador USA (LHS) Costa Rica Belize El Salvador\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1796,
      "end_char": 2032,
      "text": "Guatemala Honduras Nicaragua Panama Guatemala Honduras Nicaragua Panama\nCentral America: Exports to Mexico Central America: Imports from Mexico\n(Percent of total exports) (Percent of total imports)\n16 25\n14\n12 20\n10\n15\n8\n6 10\n4\n5\n2\n0 0\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 2032,
      "end_char": 2272,
      "text": "2005 2007 2009 2011 2013 2015 2017 2019 2021 2005 2007 2009 2011 2013 2015 2017 2019 2021\nBelize Costa Rica El Salvador Guatemala Belize Costa Rica El Salvador Guatemala\nHonduras Nicaragua Panama CA Total Honduras Nicaragua Panama CA Total\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2272,
      "end_char": 2456,
      "text": "Source: United Nations Comtrade Database. Where 2021 is not available yet, 2020 data is used.\n2. Policies supportive of trade could boost growth for the region. Mexico and its Central\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2456,
      "end_char": 2653,
      "text": "American neighbors could gain substantial benefits to growth from further trade integration as\nthere is potential to increase both inter- and intra-regional trade (IMF 2017). Given the close match\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2653,
      "end_char": 2833,
      "text": "between the top products exported by Mexico and those imported by Central America (e.g.,\nmachinery and equipment is Mexico’s second-most exported and Central America’s second-most\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2833,
      "end_char": 2965,
      "text": "imported product), there is scope for Mexico to increase exports to this region (Annex XIV Figure 2).\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 89"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 91",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 147,
      "text": "MEXICO\nAnnex XIII. Mexico’s Pension System\nMexico has reformed its pension system recently, including increases in contribution rates, a reduction\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 147,
      "end_char": 387,
      "text": "in minimum years of contributions, higher minimum pensions, and caps on fees charged. Although the\nreforms will increase pension eligibility, they might adversely affect labor market incentives and push\nmore workers in the informal market.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 387,
      "end_char": 579,
      "text": "1. Mexico has reformed its pension system significantly in recent years. Starting in 2019,\nthe means-tested social pension was replaced by a basic pension with increases in contribution rates\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 579,
      "end_char": 765,
      "text": "and adjustments to minimum pensions and minimum contribution requirements following in 2021.\nThese reforms have constituted radical changes to the pensions system envisioned in the 1997\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 765,
      "end_char": 987,
      "text": "reform and, given the lengthy transition period included in these earlier reforms, mean that the 1997\nreform was never fully operational.\n2. The 2020 reforms to the mandatory defined contributory system are underpinned by\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 987,
      "end_char": 1195,
      "text": "changes to key parameters:\n• An increase in contribution rates from 6.5–10 percent to 15 percent by 2030. Currently,\nemployees pay 1.125 percent of earnings, the government 0.225 percent (plus a social quota\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1195,
      "end_char": 1387,
      "text": "depending on earnings), and the employer 5.15 percent. This leads to a range of contribution\nrates from 6.5 percent for high earners to 10 percent for low earners. Total contributions are set\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1387,
      "end_char": 1574,
      "text": "to rise to 15 percent by increasing employer contributions to 13.875 percent for high earners,\nwhile lower earners will be subsidized by the government. The subsidies from the government\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1574,
      "end_char": 1785,
      "text": "will be concentrated on the low earners under the new rules. Government contributions will\nchange from 2023, while contribution rates for employers will rise gradually until reaching their\nfinal values in 2030.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1785,
      "end_char": 1963,
      "text": "• Lower minimum years of contributions needed to receive an earnings-related pension\nfrom 2021, with a steady increase thereafter. Until 2020, the minimum years of contributions\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1963,
      "end_char": 2151,
      "text": "was close to 25 years. The 2021 reforms lowered this to 15 years in 2021 but provide for yearly\nincreases so that a minimum of 20 years is reached by 2031. This both holds for the regular\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2151,
      "end_char": 2329,
      "text": "pension derived from the funded system as well as the minimum pension. This change has\nespecially important implications for those who are covered by the post-1997 reform system\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2329,
      "end_char": 2518,
      "text": "who are close to retirement. Under the post-1997 system around 25 years of contributions were\nrequired to receive either an annuity or a phased withdrawal meaning that the first pensioners\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2518,
      "end_char": 2702,
      "text": "who were expected to retire under this system would do so in 2023. However, with the reduction\nin minimum years of contributions introduced in 2021, some pensioners immediately became\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2702,
      "end_char": 2938,
      "text": "eligible to collect a funded pension (likely in combination with a minimum pension) rather than\nreceiving a lump-sum as expected under the old rules.\n• Significantly higher minimum pension levels. Before 2021 there was only one minimum\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2938,
      "end_char": 3122,
      "text": "pension of almost MEX$40,000 per year, to be taken at the statutory retirement age without\ndifferentiation between previous earnings levels. From 2021 the minimum pension can be taken\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00017",
      "start_char": 3122,
      "end_char": 3338,
      "text": "five years early and differs according to average career earnings (indexed to prices) and career\nlength. Moreover, the lowest minimum pension increased to MEX$45,000 for someone with 20\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 87"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 92",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 222,
      "text": "MEXICO\nyears of contributions or more while, for an average wage earner, the minimum pension more\nthan doubled to MEX$85,000 per year.\n• Caps on fees charged by AFOREs. From 2022, fees cannot exceed average fees in Chile,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 222,
      "end_char": 416,
      "text": "Colombia, and the U.S.\n3. Although the reforms are expected to have improved earnings-related pension\neligibility and produce less generous benefits than under the old pay-as-you-go system over\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 416,
      "end_char": 595,
      "text": "the medium-term, near-term pension spending will increase while labor market incentives\nmay be adversely affected. Mitigating policies, such as the phased increase in the minimum\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 595,
      "end_char": 792,
      "text": "qualifying period and the rise in contribution rates, will help to contain rising pension costs over the\nmedium-term, but near-term expenditure will rise at a time when spending pressures are high\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 792,
      "end_char": 980,
      "text": "(government spending on pensions already increased from 1.4 percent of GDP in 2000 to 4.0\npercent of GDP in 2021). Meanwhile, the adverse effects on the labor market may be long-lived. By\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 980,
      "end_char": 1175,
      "text": "enabling early retirement, the reforms could result in a tangible decline in labor force participation\namongst older workers. Moreover, the linking of the minimum pension to earnings history may\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1175,
      "end_char": 1374,
      "text": "reduce incentives to remain in (formal) employment except for those who are close to one of the\nearnings or career length thresholds. Finally, the increase in the contribution rate for employers may\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1374,
      "end_char": 1557,
      "text": "serve to increase the labor wedge, impacting employment and pushing more workers into the\nalready large informal sector despite incentives for formal participation as a result of the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1557,
      "end_char": 1660,
      "text": "government’s efforts to subsidize pension contributions for low earners.\n88 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}
